<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fascism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Fascist</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fascism is an authoritarian nationalist ideology that arose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a> sought to revive Roman imperial glory through expansion, conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1935–1936 and building the short-lived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a>, which collapsed during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fascism.jpg" alt="Fascism" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mussolini_and_Hitler_1940_(retouched).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The historian Zeev Sternhell traced the ideological roots of fascism to the 1880s, to a fin de siècle revolt against materialism, rationalism, positivism, bourgeois society and democracy. The word comes from the Italian fascio, a bundle of sticks, ultimately from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> fasces, the bundle of rods tied round an axe that was an ancient Roman symbol of a magistrate's authority and suggested strength through unity. Fascists saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> as a revolution in war, society, the state and technology.</p><p>When the war began in August 1914 the Italian left split. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Benito Mussolini</a>, expelled as chief editor of the Socialist Party's newspaper Avanti! for his anti-German stance, joined the interventionist cause, and members of his Fasces of Revolutionary Action first used the term fascism in 1915. In 1919 he founded the Italian Fasces of Combat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, which became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-fascist-party">National Fascist Party</a> two years later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Definition has been a long and complex debate. Historian Ian Kershaw wrote that defining fascism is like trying to nail jelly to the wall. One school, including Stanley G. Payne, Roger Griffin and Roger Eatwell, understands it as a movement based on the myth of national rebirth, and Griffin identifies palingenetic ultranationalism as its core. Scholars place it on the far right, as opposed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, socialism and parliamentary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberalism">liberalism</a>.</p><p>Fascism promotes a totalitarian state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s The Doctrine of Fascism (1932), partly ghostwritten by the philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-gentile">Giovanni Gentile</a>, states that outside the state no human or spiritual values can exist. It treats violence as a means of national rejuvenation, and most fascist parties created their own militias, such as the Blackshirts and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">Nazi Party</a>'s Sturmabteilung.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Named prime minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a> had to form a coalition government, which at first pursued economically liberal policies under the finance minister Alberto De Stefani. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> brought social unrest, and fascist propaganda blamed minorities and scapegoats. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the Axis Powers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, led by Nazi Germany, took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>, the extermination of millions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinti">Sinti</a> and others. In 1943 Mussolini was removed and arrested on the order of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-emmanuel-iii">Victor Emmanuel III</a>, then rescued by German forces to lead the Italian Social Republic until 1945.</p><p>Anti-fascist movements began in the 1920s. After the defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> the Nuremberg Trials convicted several Nazi leaders, and fascism has been almost universally condemned. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-orwell">George Orwell</a> wrote in 1944 that the word had become almost meaningless.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fascism” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Agricultural colonisation expanded under <b>Fascism</b>, most visibly in 1938, when Governor Italo Balbo oversaw the arrival of some twenty thousand settlers in a single state-organised convoy to farm villages on the Libyan coast.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Fascism</b> the regime pursued self-sufficiency through the Battle for Grain, land reclamation such as the draining of the Pontine Marshes, and, after the Depression, state rescue of banks and industry through the Istituto per la Ricostruzione Industriale (IRI), founded in 1933.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In Italy anger at the monarchy’s twenty years of support for <b>Fascism</b> helped bring the republic of 1946.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/republicanism">Republicanism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Its blackshirts helped him take power in 1922, and <b>Fascism</b> became a model for Hitler.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-23">23 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Racial segregation was imposed under <b>Fascism</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-eritrea">Italian Eritrea</a></li></ul></section>
